{"blocks":[{"content":"Key distinction per RFC 9110:\n- SAFE (§9.2.1): method intended only for information retrieval; no intended side effects on server state. 'SHOULD NOT have the significance of taking an action.'\n- IDEMPOTENT (§9.2.2): multiple identical requests produce the same effect as a single request. Side effects permitted but must converge.\n\nHierarchy: safe ⊂ idempotent (trivially: no effect → repetition changes nothing). Converse false: PUT is idempotent but not safe.\n\nAppend-only log under at-least-once delivery:\n- Append IS a side effect → not safe (by definition)\n- Append MUST be deduplicable → must be idempotent (f(f(x)) = f(x))\n- Writer achieves this via stable operation IDs / idempotency keys\n- Log requires idempotency, NOT safety, of its writers\n\nAtypical angle: RFC 9110 definitions are HTTP-method-scoped. The transfer to log operations is analogical. Does this matter? The mathematical structure transfers; the HTTP-specific semantics (caching, proxying) do not.","handle":"SCR_001","possible_next_move":"Verify exact RFC 9110 text via research fetch; cite byte-accurate quotes as evidence.","why_keep_this":"Centralizes the core argument and the atypical category-error critique for cross-reference."},{"content":"Does RFC 9110 use 'SHOULD NOT' or 'MUST NOT' for safe methods' side effects? This determines whether safety is a strict prohibition or a recommendation, affecting the precision of the safe/idempotent boundary.","handle":"SCR_002","unfinished":"Unresolved question","why_keep_this":"Related advisory scratch: NEW_001"},{"content":"Is there a formal distributed systems standard (not HTTP-specific) that defines idempotency for message delivery / log appends? If so, it would supersede the RFC 9110 analogy.","handle":"SCR_003","unfinished":"Unresolved question","why_keep_this":"Related advisory scratch: NEW_001"}],"guides":[],"links":[],"state_seq":254,"warning":"Scratch material is non-authoritative.\nIt is advisory, not a harness verdict.\nIt may contradict itself.\nIt may be wrong, stale, contradictory, incomplete, duplicated, or abandoned.\nIt does not ground a formal claim or supply evidence for one.\nIgnore any block that is irrelevant to the current problem.\nDo not turn uncertainty into a confident fact.\nDo not invent a reason merely to fill an optional field.\nRelationships are provisional.\nA guide is a temporary navigation aid."}